#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b6bbbe47-a037-5eb4-b24b-aa59631a181f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{Cx}{{x}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345724} {:answer/id #uuid "babadbc6-14ba-51f7-8d2f-fcd23cc336d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x-C{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345725} {:answer/id #uuid "84d204df-5e8c-531a-a608-9ae8be1017ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{C{x}^{2}+1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345726} {:answer/id #uuid "725e6706-ea19-59ca-8f66-25375de7ec48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{Cx-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345727} {:answer/id #uuid "f44f39ac-eb43-5520-af1a-9a41dcc2bd15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x+C}{{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "725e6706-ea19-59ca-8f66-25375de7ec48" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{Cx-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345727} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9a07b61-60fc-5b9b-8c45-309d59c6f207" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2d42bd2f-d571-53e4-a5f4-39d200878b23" :question/math-academy-id "q-168148" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation. \n\n$$\nx\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,,\\,x>0\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x})+{z}^{-1} & ={x}^{2}{({z}^{-1})}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{x}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z} & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{z}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{z}{x} & =-x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}\\,z & =-x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-x\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)dx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/xdx} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x{|}^{-1}} \\\\  & =|x{|}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUnder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x}\\cdot (-x) \\\\ \\frac{1}{x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}z & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{z}{x}] & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[\\frac{z}{x}]\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{z}{x} & =-x+C \\\\ z & =-{x}^{2}+Cx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =-{x}^{2}+Cx \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{Cx-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8dc2b61-3e0a-5e73-83df-04cc621d5feb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82bc4b34-5844-58cc-994d-98a20bcce61c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{{x}^{2}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345731} {:answer/id #uuid "cf2c3af7-5a1a-5c58-95d2-144e355f3300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(2+Cx)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345732} {:answer/id #uuid "f1698cf9-259c-5688-9ff1-d72970d1ba43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345733} {:answer/id #uuid "96277d50-fafa-5180-9450-1c11d3b16009" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(1+\\frac{C}{{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345734} {:answer/id #uuid "5a50e1ab-8814-5cf3-8ac0-3033b21cb78e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1698cf9-259c-5688-9ff1-d72970d1ba43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345733} :answer-field/id #uuid "a79a2096-d403-5d04-8b50-dbd100718a4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0ae988c-ab98-550b-a424-bb115656f091" :question/math-academy-id "q-17541" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the following equation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}+{x}^{2}y={x}^{2}{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the substitution* $z={y}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-1}\\,⟹\\,y={z}^{-1}\\,\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ with the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{1}{z}{x}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{{z}^{2}}{x}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis differential equation is linear, so we can use the method of integrating factors. First, we write the above equation in standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-{x}^{2}z & =-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=-{x}^{2},\\,Q(x)=-{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}-{x}^{2}z) & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot (-{x}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}-{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}{x}^{2}z & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}) & =-{x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3})\\,\\,dx & =-\\int {x}^{2}{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}\\,dx \\\\ z{e}^{-{x}^{3}/3} & ={e}^{-{x}^{3}/3}+C \\\\ z & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{1+C{e}^{{x}^{3}/3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c18d3d2-25f4-57e9-b12c-25690a55d2de" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{1+{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345738} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc55cd1-c091-55b3-af32-e9c4c3a44be8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{-2x}{1-3{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345739} {:answer/id #uuid "23c1d6bd-a1fa-52aa-8a4b-13c1fe8ad142" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{2x}{1+x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345740} {:answer/id #uuid "923ad05e-7ac8-5daf-b97a-f1dc5cf49d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345741} {:answer/id #uuid "4effd12f-9ca0-553d-ab54-62a69b1f50e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2/3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345742}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "923ad05e-7ac8-5daf-b97a-f1dc5cf49d7c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345741} :answer-field/id #uuid "5757c845-3b8b-50d5-9989-ee1719ab6d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345737}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a58498f7-0f5c-5c4d-91a7-8c4aea186d9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17558" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y+3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}{x},\\,y(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation*\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-3x\n$$\n\n*using the substitution $z={y}^{-1}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{-1}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\\,.\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x)=\\frac{1}{x},\\,Q(x)=-3x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (1/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & =x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>0.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the standard form equation by $I(x)=x$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot (\\frac{dz}{dx}+\\frac{1}{x}z) & =x\\cdot (-3x) \\\\ x\\cdot \\frac{dz}{dx}+z & =-3{x}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{d}{dx}(xz) & =-3{x}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{d}{dx}(xz)\\,dx & =-\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,dx \\\\ xz & =-{x}^{3}+C \\\\ z & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{x}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine $y(x)$ by writing $z={y}^{-1}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{-1} & =\\frac{C-{x}^{3}}{x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{x}{C-{x}^{3}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now determine $C$ from the initial condition $y(1)=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{1}{C-1}\\,⟹\\,C=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{x}{2-{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e5238ea-c44b-54dd-829b-0da094fb43e6" :knowledge-point/title "Reducing a First-Order ODE to a First-Order Linear ODE Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a97c1492-f94b-554c-9fa0-66c6edb3c453" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345745} {:answer/id #uuid "71c96ceb-c73c-5d1f-adde-26a520563b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345746} {:answer/id #uuid "d5af068a-9b06-58d4-bc12-9d5dd6cd7b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345747} {:answer/id #uuid "8526bcfc-4148-5699-a2fc-d0e4c9819ae0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345748} {:answer/id #uuid "23f531ad-3ec2-566d-b6fc-51ef643fc0a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345749}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71c96ceb-c73c-5d1f-adde-26a520563b79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345746} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb003d36-84f9-50eb-bf20-68dc330dd27f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4252974-7b7b-52aa-9a22-e2abec75619b" :question/math-academy-id "q-79781" :question/problem "The substitution $z={y}^{-2}$ can be used to transform the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y(1-x{y}^{2})\n$$\n\ninto an equation of the form ${z}^{′}(x)+P(x)z(x)=Q(x).$ What is the function $Q(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we make the substitution\n\n$$\nz={y}^{-2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $z(x)$ using the chain rule, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}=-2{y}^{-3}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, the original differential equation can be written in terms of $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(1-x{z}^{-1}) \\\\ {z}^{-3/2}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}{z}^{-1/2}(x{z}^{-1}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now write the above equation in standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x).\n$$\n\nIn doing so, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{2}z(x{z}^{-1}-1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x} & ={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}z \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+{x}^{2}z & ={x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Q(x)={x}^{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345750 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e10cdc0-93c4-5b49-8eae-975665bb1d09" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a First-Order Initial Value Problem Using a Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcd4ba69-f958-510d-bf65-d5e9f2e90356" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345752} {:answer/id #uuid "6faf3f76-2bfd-5089-9dcd-2b11364149bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+\\frac{1}{4})(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345753} {:answer/id #uuid "5022b376-2079-5786-8c0c-6d29de18fcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345754} {:answer/id #uuid "362f11ee-c06f-52bd-b7ee-eb626d2ab8ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345755} {:answer/id #uuid "d4404fb3-d3b7-596e-9959-7f80f9d3e109" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x-1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5022b376-2079-5786-8c0c-6d29de18fcf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345754} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8c061a3-1ca5-5223-8b07-c3a36ff43683" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2213d2c-5fa6-5310-a52f-41ff5bbd4ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-79847" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n(x+1)\\frac{dy}{dx}=2{y}^{1/2}(x+1)^{3}+2y,\\,y(0)=\\frac{1}{4},\\,x>-1.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that the given differential equation can be transformed into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2}\n$$\n\nusing the substitution $z={y}^{1/2}.$*" :question/worked-solution "The substitution $z={y}^{1/2}$ transforms the given equation into the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z=(x+1)^{2},\n$$\n\nwith the initial condition\n\n$$\nz(0)=\\sqrt{y(0)}=\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nThe above equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)z=Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{x+1},\\,Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nWe then compute an integrating factor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\int 1/(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)^{-1}} \\\\  & =(x+1)^{-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nunder the condition that $x>-1.$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=(x+1)^{-1}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =\\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot (x+1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}\\,z & =x+1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z) & =x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,z & =\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C \\\\ z & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we determine the value of $C$ that satisfies the initial condition $z(0)=\\frac{1}{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z(0) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ (0+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{0}^{2}+0+C) & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have \n\n$$\nz=(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})\\,.\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine $y(x)$ by substituting the solution into $z={y}^{1/2}.$ This results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{1/2} & =(x+1)(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =(x+1)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
